 {"id": "DF-BOARDCTL-44", "title": "[P2] --repair-events re-encodes each renumbered event through map[string]any and silently mutates NON-id fields (ints >2^53 round, double spaces collapse inside string values, keys reorder) — README promises 'every other field untouched'", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "reasoning": "Dogfood run 25 (tick boardctl-dogfood-2026-10-23-10-52-34), reproduced at HEAD 900b7b5 and on the RELEASED v0.1.11 binary on a fresh bunker box. Fixture events.jsonl (3 events, ids 10,9,11; the 9 descends) with a renumbered line carrying 'detail':'cmd: rsync -a  /x  /y', 'title':'fix  double  spaces', 'tick_number':9007199254740993, keys z_first before a_last. After `boardctl normalize-board --repair-events --apply` the line reads { \"a_last\": \"z\", ..., \"detail\": \"cmd: rsync -a /x /y\", ..., \"id\": 11, ..., \"tick_number\": 9007199254740992, ..., \"title\": \"fix double spaces\", \"z_first\": \"a\" } — the double spaces inside TWO string VALUES were collapsed (the recorded command and the title no longer match what was written), the 2^53+1 integer was rounded DOWN to 2^53, and key order was rewritten alphabetically. LIVE evidence: a scratch copy of the real task-router board (700 events, 473 descending ids) repaired with --apply → exactly 1 of the 473 renumbered events lost non-id bytes (events.jsonl line 691, its detail JSON body re-indented 2→1 spaces: '\"tick_id\": ...' went from '\\n  \"' to '\\n \"'). Code: internal/board/repair_events.go:224-250 renumberEventID unmarshals into map[string]any (float64 for every number, sorted keys on re-marshal) and then runs bytes.ReplaceAll(out, []byte('  '), []byte(' ')) over the WHOLE re-encoded line, which reaches inside string values. This contradicts README ~line 878 ('leaves every other field on the event untouched') and the file's own header comment (repair_events.go:15 'preserve every field except \"id\"'). Fix direction: surgical id-only byte rewrite on the raw line (locate the id token) or, at minimum, decode with json.Decoder+UseNumber and drop the global double-space normalization.", "created_at": "2026-10-23T11:23:17Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-23T20:59:14Z", "created_by": "dogfood-boardctl-run25", "acceptance_criteria": "1) `boardctl normalize-board --repair-events --apply` on a fixture whose descending event carries 'title':'fix  double  spaces', 'detail':'cmd: rsync -a  /x  /y', 'tick_number':9007199254740993 and a deliberately non-alphabetical key order must leave those values and that key order byte-identical: a `diff` of the before/after line must show only the `id` token changed, and the double spaces must still be present (grep -c 'double  spaces' events.jsonl prints 1). 2) A regression test under internal/board covering all three shapes (double-space string, integer above 2^53, key order) must exit 1 on the pre-fix tree and exit 0 after the fix: run `go test ./internal/board -run RepairEvents` and record both exit codes in the row's evidence. 3) Re-running the repair on a copy of task-router's board must show id-only changes: `git diff` of the pre- and post-repair events.jsonl prints zero lines whose changed field is not `id`. 4) Full suite exits 0: `go test ./...` expected exit code 0.", "capability_tags": [], "complexity": 3, "depends_on": [], "foreman_note": "Foreman wave task 1; implementation branch pushed and merged; verdict .gitreins/history/2026-10-23/6529b431/verdict.json.", "commit_hash": "e9d50f313cb20b0169857ec0243c65aa2ab77934", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "Raw-byte id-only event repair landed in de3383d and merged as e9d50f3; focused RepairEvents tests RED->GREEN, full Go gates passed, task-router 704-event live copy changed only id bytes; GitReins PASS 6529b431.", "detail": {"fingerprint": "17ce981adb436ef60e957b399ca3e74b3cbeb8c7577b26a55a688fd0b6ca1bca", "evidence": [{"run_id":"6529b431","ts":"2026-10-23T20:59:14Z"}]}, "worker_status": "complete", "completed_at": "2026-10-23 20:50:00", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/boardctl-DF-BOARDCTL-44", "branch": "wt/DF-BOARDCTL-44"}
-{"id": "DF-BOARDCTL-45", "title": "[P3] --repair-events can CREATE a duplicate event id (renumber target never checked against ids later in the file) and still reports 'duplicate ids: 0'", "status": "pending", "priority": "P3", "reasoning": "Dogfood run 25, HEAD 900b7b5 and released v0.1.11 (identical behaviour). Fixture events.jsonl with ids 10, 9, 11 in file order: line 2 (id 9) descends below 10, so the repair renumbers it to runningMax+1 = 11 — an id that line 3 already carries. The command then reports `duplicate ids: 0` (it counted duplicates in the INPUT only) and `validate` prints `[warn] events.jsonl line 3: duplicate event id 11 (also line 2)` with `RESULT: OK`. Net effect: the repair's own census claims a clean input and says nothing about the output, while the file now has two events sharing one id — any consumer keyed on event id (watchdogs, dedupe-by-id, cross-board sync) sees an ambiguous record, and the id that used to name one specific event now names two. Code: internal/board/repair_events.go:145-180 — renumber uses a left-to-right running max and never consults the ids that appear LATER in the file.", "created_at": "2026-10-23T11:23:17Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-23T11:23:17Z", "created_by": "dogfood-boardctl-run25", "acceptance_criteria": "1) Fixture ids 10,9,11 must repair to a file where no two events share an id (renumber to 12, or base the renumber on the maximum numeric id over the whole file) and the report's duplicate count is computed on the OUTPUT. 2) `boardctl validate` on the repaired fixture prints no 'duplicate event id' line. 3) A regression test asserts both the uniqueness of the output ids and the reported duplicate count for this fixture. 4) `go test ./...` exits 0.", "capability_tags": [], "complexity": 3, "depends_on": [], "foreman_note": null, "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "detail": {"fingerprint": "1e544bcf138451833b636a110ca84da12ef5b6cf848ede17d0df66651e2ccb5b"}}
