 {"id": "DF-BOARDCTL-46", "title": "[P3] normalize-board hard-fails on ONE unparseable line and names no remedy — and --skip-bad-lines on the same command is a dead end (refuses to write), so 400+ rows of drift stay unrepaired", "status": "complete", "priority": "P3", "reasoning": "Dogfood run 25, HEAD 900b7b5. Live fleet condition: warpfs's board at /home/kara/warpfs/.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl line 412 (row INT-CI-011, written by the warpfs foreman 2026-10-23T10:33Z) carries a duplicate JSON key `worker_status`. On a scratch copy of that board: `boardctl -C <copy> normalize-board` → rc=1, `boardctl: .../tasks.jsonl: line 412: duplicate key \"worker_status\" in row` — no hint about what to do; `normalize-board --skip-bad-lines` (a flag the command's own help documents) prints SKIPPED-LINES with the line, then `boardctl: board read was degraded: unparseable lines were skipped` and writes NOTHING — so the 410 parseable rows of key/status/depends_on drift stay unrepaired and the user is at a dead end twice. The documented chain that DOES work is buried in the README's 'Damaged-board salvage (DF-BOARDCTL-9)' section: `validate --repair` wrote 417 salvaged rows to tasks.rewritten.jsonl and dropped exactly line 412, tasks.jsonl untouched — verified this run. A user following the normalize-board help text never learns that.", "created_at": "2026-10-23T11:23:17Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-23T20:59:14Z", "created_by": "dogfood-boardctl-run25", "acceptance_criteria": "1) The unparseable-line refusal from normalize-board names the remedy inline (line number + `validate --repair` → review tasks.rewritten.jsonl → swap → re-run normalize-board). 2) Either `--skip-bad-lines` on normalize-board becomes a usable degraded path that writes the repaired tasks.jsonl and preserves the dropped lines in a sidecar (e.g. tasks.skipped-lines.jsonl) with an explicit exit code, or the flag is removed from that command's help and the help points at validate --repair. 3) A fixture reproducing